# Rule C4: one redirect hop, straight to the final URL.
#
# http://www.devhub.my/x/ was costing two: Netlify forces HTTPS at the edge
# first (301), then applies the primary-domain redirect (301). An explicit rule
# with the full source URL is matched before both, so the wrong-scheme +
# wrong-host case collapses into a single hop like the other three variants.
#
# Absolute URLs are only meaningful in the `from` field for this purpose — do
# not rewrite the path rules below to use them.
http://www.devhub.my/*    https://devhub.my/:splat    301!
https://www.devhub.my/*   https://devhub.my/:splat    301!

# Marketing downloads (ebooks) — proxied from the private devhub-downloads
# site. Actual paths are unguessable tokens managed outside this repo; the
# links are shared by the marketing team only and must never be listed on
# the site, in robots.txt, or in the sitemap.
/dl/*    https://devhub-downloads.netlify.app/:splat    200

# The CMS is its own page in the build (dist/admin/index.html), not a route in
# the SPA. The redirect (not a rewrite) is deliberate: the page's stylesheet and
# Sveltia's own config.yml lookup are relative to the document, so the URL has
# to keep its trailing slash.
/admin     /admin/             301
/admin/    /admin/index.html   200

# Legacy URLs. One hop each, straight to the final trailing-slash URL — rule C4
# allows no chains, so these point at the destination and never at another
# redirect.
#
# /classes was replaced by /trainings.
/classes         /trainings/    301
/classes/*       /trainings/    301

# Posts moved from /blog/ to /resources/articles/, the path the SOP's URL map
# assigns to Article entities. The slug is unchanged, so every shared link
# survives as a single 301 to the same post. These stay permanently: the whole
# reason the move happened now, at zero organic traffic, is that the cost of
# breaking a link only goes up.
/blog            /resources/                301
/blog/           /resources/                301
/blog/:slug      /resources/articles/:slug/ 301

# There is deliberately NO `/* /index.html 200` fallback any more.
#
# That single rule was the root cause of most of the 1 Sep 2026 audit findings:
# it answered every unmatched path with the homepage at HTTP 200, which is why
# /about/, /services/, /llms.txt and /tidak-wujud/ all returned the same
# document, why the crawler reported "5 pages" for a nineteen-URL sitemap, and
# why every URL canonicalised to https://devhub.my.
#
# Every route is now a real file written by scripts/prerender.mjs, so Netlify
# serves the right document directly. Anything that matches no file falls
# through to dist/404.html, which Netlify returns with a genuine 404 status.
